WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.583
(upbeat music)

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:08.740
- Welcome to another
episode of The Addy Hour,

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:11.880
our 20th episode, which
I'm very grateful about.

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:13.920
We've been doing this since
about February of this year.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:16.360
So it's really been nice to
see this come to fruition

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:17.620
in a lot of ways.

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:19.960
Today, I'm honored to be able
to host another conversation

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:21.280
on a really important topic.

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:23.830
We're gonna be talking
about depression, ketamine,

00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:26.260
and just important
considerations about navigating

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:28.420
the mental health care system.

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:30.273
So as usual on this podcast,

00:00:30.273 --> 00:00:32.260
some really important, some heavy topics,

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:34.140
but it's also gonna be
encouraging, I think as well,

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:37.610
just as we talk through
some of these experiences

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:38.500
from our two guests,

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:41.390
both from a provider
standpoint and from someone

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:42.680
who's walked through the system

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:44.940
and they're actually working
together as colleagues

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:46.620
in this moment as well.

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:49.220
So I'll let them elaborate
on that as we go through.

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:52.080
But I'm honored to be
able to welcome two guests

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:53.600
to the podcast today.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:57.030
The first of which is Dr. Gerry Sanacora.

00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:00.170
Dr. Sanacora is the Gross
Professor of Psychiatry

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:01.480
at the Yale School of Medicine.

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:02.640
He's also the director

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:04.890
of the Yale Depression
and Research program

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:05.950
and the co-director

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:08.270
of the Interventional Psychiatry Program

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:10.480
at Yale New Haven Hospital.

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:12.200
He's someone who's been
engaged in research

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:15.640
for over 25 years using both
pre-clinical rodent models

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:17.760
and also human clinical studies

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:19.870
to really understand the biology

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:21.770
of neuropsychiatric illnesses.

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:24.310
And also to really think about
and develop new approaches

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:26.110
for treating and
preventing those illnesses

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:27.990
in the first place.

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:30.430
He's someone who's led a
lot of different projects,

00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:32.580
projects within the National
Institutes of Health,

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:35.230
projects have been
sponsored by foundations,

00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:37.000
projects that have been
sponsored within the industry.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:39.650
So it has a lot of experiences
in different realms.

00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:42.400
And what is really impressive
is that he's also worked

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:45.560
on creating and disseminating
educational resources

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:47.070
and also providing leadership

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:49.800
to both national and
international consensus

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:52.450
on international consensus statements.

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:53.960
He's won numerous awards for his work.

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:54.793
He's a fellow

00:01:54.793 --> 00:01:57.160
of the American college of
Neuropsychopharmacology.

00:01:57.160 --> 00:01:58.240
He also received

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:01.300
the Anna-Monika Stiftung
International Award

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:03.820
and received a Joel Elkes Research Award.

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:06.160
So I'm grateful for the work
that he does in this space

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:07.930
and honored to welcome Dr. Gerry Sanacora

00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:09.313
to The Addy Hour podcast.

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:11.337
- Thank you, Nii.

00:02:11.337 --> 00:02:12.370
It's really a pleasure to be here.

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:13.970
- Thanks so much for being here.

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:17.600
My second guest is Ashley Clayton.

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:19.800
And Ashley is someone
who describes herself

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:22.210
as a person who loves stories,

00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:26.650
as an avid reader of poetry,
memoirs and nonfiction.

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:29.440
She also finds joy and comfort
in music and impressively,

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:31.160
during the pandemic, has picked up

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:33.880
and has been teaching herself the banjo.

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:34.950
Ashley is someone who grew up

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:36.530
in the Rolling Hills of Kentucky,

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:38.960
moved to Connecticut for grad
school and has been living

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:41.730
in Connecticut for the past 12 years.

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:43.840
She has a master's degree
in community psychology

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:45.890
and is also a research associate

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:47.090
in the Department of Psychiatry

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:48.770
at the Yale School of Medicine.

00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:51.280
She initially ventured into
the community psychology space

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:53.960
through a determination to
use her firsthand experience

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:56.010
with mental illness for the greater good.

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:59.140
And she's also very
dedicated to social justice.

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:02.110
So she's a mental health
advocate, or activist rather.

00:03:02.110 --> 00:03:04.470
She's also published
numerous research papers

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:06.150
on social inclusion of individuals

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:07.820
living with severe mental illness,

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:09.530
on maternal mental health,

00:03:09.530 --> 00:03:12.320
on recovery-oriented and
person-centered care,

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:15.720
and has also written healthcare
narratives and essays.

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:18.270
She's on the advisory board
of different organizations.

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:19.870
Also owns a consulting business

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:21.660
where she provides consultation

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:24.580
in program evaluation
and patient engagement.

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:25.950
So I'm deeply grateful

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:28.160
for all the important work that she does.

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:30.440
And also thankful that she's
taken time to join us here

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:31.723
on The Addy Hour podcast.

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:34.870
- Yeah, thank you so much for having me.

00:03:34.870 --> 00:03:37.164
I'm looking forward to our conversation.

00:03:37.164 --> 00:03:37.997
- Definitely.

00:03:37.997 --> 00:03:38.830
Definitely looking forward to it

00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:40.260
and grateful to both of you.

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:43.372
This, in some sense, is
an in-house conversation

00:03:43.372 --> 00:03:45.340
since we're all at Yale,

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:47.660
but also we wanna make sure as
we always do on this podcast,

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:50.337
that we're just communicating
to the general public as well.

00:03:50.337 --> 00:03:52.510
And so I'm grateful for the ways

00:03:52.510 --> 00:03:53.960
you all have done that already.

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:55.210
And I know that this conversation

00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:57.760
is gonna continue to do that as well.

00:03:57.760 --> 00:03:59.400
But as I always do on the podcast,

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:00.233
I always like to check in

00:04:00.233 --> 00:04:02.370
and see how people are doing overall.

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:07.370
Especially as we wrap up 2020,
as we look forward to 2021.

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:09.100
And Ashley, why don't we
go ahead and start with you

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:11.350
and just see how you're
doing in this moment?

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:13.840
- Yeah.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:17.080
I think the most honest answer
to that is I am both okay

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:18.903
and very much not okay.

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:22.247
So I think, the last, we're coming

00:04:22.247 --> 00:04:25.793
into this two years of a pandemic.

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:30.793
And I think we're all sharing that stress.

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:35.340
There's a low level or
sometimes a high level

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:38.190
of just general
overwhelmed stress fatigue.

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:40.350
So definitely, feeling that.

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:44.780
I think my life during
the pandemic personally

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:48.902
has been challenging aside from everything

00:04:48.902 --> 00:04:51.620
we're all experiencing together.

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:53.990
The day that Connecticut shut down.

00:04:53.990 --> 00:04:57.330
My brother, they found the second tumor.

00:04:57.330 --> 00:04:59.094
He had a glioblastoma.

00:04:59.094 --> 00:05:04.094
So at the exact moment
that everything happened,

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:08.490
our world, my family
life got pretty chaotic.

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:11.310
And so he died earlier this year

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:16.310
and that's been very painful and sad.

00:05:16.370 --> 00:05:20.100
So I've been in also a process
of mourning and grieving,

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:21.890
but also being away from my family,

00:05:21.890 --> 00:05:24.260
not being there as much as I wanna be.

00:05:24.260 --> 00:05:29.260
So, and in 2020 I had
three job transitions,

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:32.423
which is stressful.

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:37.990
I've had some pretty
strange health issues,

00:05:37.990 --> 00:05:39.110
physical health issues.

00:05:39.110 --> 00:05:40.270
So dealing with those.

00:05:40.270 --> 00:05:45.270
But I also feel okay in some weird ways.

00:05:45.590 --> 00:05:47.570
So I think the most honest answer

00:05:47.570 --> 00:05:49.690
is really I'm okay and not okay.

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:53.150
And I think a lot of
people can relate to that.

00:05:53.150 --> 00:05:53.983
- Yeah.

00:05:53.983 --> 00:05:56.450
And I think that's such an important piece

00:05:56.450 --> 00:05:57.283
to just get across.

00:05:57.283 --> 00:05:58.410
I mean, just the level of candor

00:05:58.410 --> 00:06:00.173
that you just shared
as well with everything

00:06:00.173 --> 00:06:02.750
that you've gone through
in the last two years.

00:06:02.750 --> 00:06:05.800
And the fact that you can have
both of those simultaneously,

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:08.420
being okay and not okay,
I think a lot of ways,

00:06:08.420 --> 00:06:09.900
and people have heard this
on this program before,

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:12.940
but that's validating, I think
for a lot of people to hear.

00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:14.680
'Cause sometimes, I think people feel

00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:15.640
stuck in one or the other.

00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:17.713
Like either I have to not be doing well.

00:06:19.318 --> 00:06:21.010
People always have room to have both

00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:22.353
and I think that's really
important knowledge

00:06:22.353 --> 00:06:23.860
that you're sharing that.

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:25.160
So really appreciate that.

00:06:26.350 --> 00:06:27.183
Gerry, what about you?

00:06:27.183 --> 00:06:29.410
How are you doing in this moment as well?

00:06:29.410 --> 00:06:34.160
- Ashley captured it extremely concisely

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:35.480
very well in many ways.

00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:37.520
And it's been a difficult,

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:40.270
Professionally, it's been a
hard year, as you can imagine.

00:06:40.270 --> 00:06:44.810
It's been extremely busy and
trying to adapt to working

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:48.540
in environments that
we're not prepared to work

00:06:48.540 --> 00:06:49.470
and doing so much.

00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:52.040
By telemedicine and a lot
of the treatments we give

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:53.740
are actually hands-on
and trying to figure out

00:06:53.740 --> 00:06:55.180
how to do that.

00:06:55.180 --> 00:06:56.720
When the volumes are so high,

00:06:56.720 --> 00:07:00.980
but yet all the procedures
that we need to ensure safety

00:07:00.980 --> 00:07:03.090
become so much more time-consuming.

00:07:03.090 --> 00:07:05.275
It's been a struggle, but overall,

00:07:05.275 --> 00:07:09.340
I have to consider myself
one of the real lucky people

00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:12.678
that has not been directly
personally affected

00:07:12.678 --> 00:07:14.193
by this pandemic.

00:07:14.193 --> 00:07:17.083
And that has been extremely fortunate.

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:22.490
The burden has not been shared evenly

00:07:22.490 --> 00:07:24.470
from everybody that see.

00:07:24.470 --> 00:07:26.270
- Yeah, that's very true as well.

00:07:26.270 --> 00:07:27.540
And again, so much, Ashley,

00:07:27.540 --> 00:07:29.640
I just appreciate the way
you shared that, honestly,

00:07:29.640 --> 00:07:30.930
'cause I think sometimes as well,

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:32.600
and that's also come up that people

00:07:32.600 --> 00:07:34.700
who haven't been directly affected

00:07:34.700 --> 00:07:37.410
sometimes lessen the challenges
they have also faced.

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:39.840
So, I mean, for you as a provider,

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:41.350
obviously, that's also very challenging.

00:07:41.350 --> 00:07:43.040
But at the same time, as you mentioned,

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:46.030
you do have a place of
thankfulness being blessed

00:07:46.030 --> 00:07:48.040
and privileged to not have
been directly affected.

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:50.580
So I appreciate you putting
that in context as well.

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:52.740
And again, even as we're starting,

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:54.900
that's one of the reasons
why I was so I'm grateful

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:55.733
that both of you have joined.

00:07:55.733 --> 00:07:58.060
Just a level of candor
with which you both speak

00:07:58.060 --> 00:07:59.350
and the honesty and the realness,

00:07:59.350 --> 00:08:01.640
I think goes a very, very long way.

00:08:01.640 --> 00:08:03.140
Another question that I wanted to ask,

00:08:03.140 --> 00:08:04.920
I may allude to this in the introduction,

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:07.330
but you all have had a
shift in your relationship

00:08:07.330 --> 00:08:08.876
over the years,

00:08:08.876 --> 00:08:09.794
but I also just want to start

00:08:09.794 --> 00:08:10.840
and ask how the two of you met.

00:08:10.840 --> 00:08:13.960
In what context and how that
relationship has changed

00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:14.890
and developed over the years.

00:08:14.890 --> 00:08:17.250
So whoever wants, I know best person

00:08:17.250 --> 00:08:18.420
to ask to introduce that,

00:08:18.420 --> 00:08:21.573
but I'll let you all decide, I suppose.

00:08:22.890 --> 00:08:23.723
- Sure.

00:08:23.723 --> 00:08:24.790
Is it okay if I go?

00:08:24.790 --> 00:08:25.623
Oh yeah, definitely.

00:08:25.623 --> 00:08:27.036
- Please do.

00:08:27.036 --> 00:08:31.617
- So I met Gerry in early part of 2016.

00:08:34.070 --> 00:08:38.260
So I was hanging out with a
colleague, our mutual colleague,

00:08:38.260 --> 00:08:40.400
but we're all colleagues so, of ours.

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:41.680
And I had really, at this point,

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:45.050
been struggling really
severely with depression

00:08:45.050 --> 00:08:46.520
for the last few years,

00:08:46.520 --> 00:08:48.430
but was the first time,
I was actually having

00:08:48.430 --> 00:08:50.323
what we call functional impairments.

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:55.000
I was having some cognitive
deficits struggling at work

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:57.220
just look at my spreadsheet and be like,

00:08:57.220 --> 00:09:00.293
I don't know what to do.

00:09:01.792 --> 00:09:04.640
And so actually was on a
partial medical leave from work,

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:06.940
which was the first time
I ever needed to do that.

00:09:06.940 --> 00:09:08.540
And so I had reached out to a colleague

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:12.270
who does some research with ketamine

00:09:12.270 --> 00:09:14.000
in a different area than depression,

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:14.833
and just said, hey,

00:09:14.833 --> 00:09:16.090
I remember you saying something

00:09:16.090 --> 00:09:18.130
about ketamine and depression,

00:09:18.130 --> 00:09:19.500
could you meet and talk?

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:22.620
And so he actually referred
me to Gerry who at the time

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:27.210
was running a phase two clinical trial.

00:09:27.210 --> 00:09:31.995
And so, he made a call on
my behalf later that week.

00:09:31.995 --> 00:09:34.060
I met with Gerry to really talk

00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:38.240
about his research projects and to see,

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:39.470
am I a good candidate

00:09:39.470 --> 00:09:41.820
for any of the studies he has going on?

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:43.610
And is it something that if I am,

00:09:43.610 --> 00:09:45.000
I would wanna move forward with?

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:49.040
And so that's really how we met first

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:54.040
and kind of a principal
investigator, physician,

00:09:54.170 --> 00:09:58.760
patient research, research
participant relationship.

00:09:58.760 --> 00:10:02.770
And I did end up participating
in some clinical trials

00:10:02.770 --> 00:10:03.750
throughout that year.

00:10:03.750 --> 00:10:08.750
And he was involved
after the study is ended

00:10:08.990 --> 00:10:13.990
and the transition of my care
over to a team I have now.

00:10:16.310 --> 00:10:18.196
But in the last few years,

00:10:18.196 --> 00:10:21.890
we've really established our
relationship as colleagues,

00:10:21.890 --> 00:10:24.820
as people who really wanna talk publicly

00:10:24.820 --> 00:10:26.560
and have to have good conversations

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:31.110
about what is it like to
live with severe depression,

00:10:31.110 --> 00:10:33.880
what treatments are available,

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:35.250
all of these different things together.

00:10:35.250 --> 00:10:37.680
That's a path I think
we really wanted to do.

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:42.680
So we've separated our
doctor-patient relationship really,

00:10:42.757 --> 00:10:46.300
and have established a firm
relationship as colleagues

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:48.524
here in the university, so.

00:10:48.524 --> 00:10:50.410
- I look at it from the perspective,

00:10:50.410 --> 00:10:53.230
there are many good doctors
that could do what I was doing

00:10:53.230 --> 00:10:56.080
to help Ashley, but there are
very few people like Ashley

00:10:56.080 --> 00:10:59.470
that could really help
us understand better

00:10:59.470 --> 00:11:02.250
how to provide the best
care we can to patients.

00:11:02.250 --> 00:11:06.810
And I think our collaboration
through this sentence

00:11:06.810 --> 00:11:09.210
is real important.

00:11:09.210 --> 00:11:12.283
And I know you do have
good care elsewhere.

00:11:14.380 --> 00:11:17.710
There are more good doctors
than there are people like you.

00:11:17.710 --> 00:11:18.543
- Thank you.

00:11:18.543 --> 00:11:19.376
- Wow, wow.

00:11:19.376 --> 00:11:21.610
Yeah, that's really powerful
I think in a lot of ways.

00:11:21.610 --> 00:11:25.060
And just to be able to have,
I mean, I'm speculating here,

00:11:25.060 --> 00:11:27.770
but I would say that kind
of relationship is rare.

00:11:27.770 --> 00:11:29.766
To have that collegial relationship

00:11:29.766 --> 00:11:32.030
and then people who are mutually invested

00:11:32.030 --> 00:11:34.670
and willing to learn from
each perspective as well.

00:11:34.670 --> 00:11:36.070
And I think that goes,
we'll get into this as well,

00:11:36.070 --> 00:11:39.290
but that probably goes a long
way into helping both sides

00:11:39.290 --> 00:11:41.633
see blind spots that we might
not have seen otherwise,

00:11:41.633 --> 00:11:43.780
I think is really important.

00:11:43.780 --> 00:11:46.130
I did wanna pick up Ashley on one aspect

00:11:46.130 --> 00:11:46.963
that you mentioned too,

00:11:46.963 --> 00:11:50.220
in terms of walking
through severe depression.

00:11:50.220 --> 00:11:52.590
Because I think that's a conversation

00:11:52.590 --> 00:11:53.890
that's really important.

00:11:53.890 --> 00:11:56.534
That has talked about
in society has come up

00:11:56.534 --> 00:11:57.880
in this podcast as well.

00:11:57.880 --> 00:12:00.750
But I think sometimes from
a general perspectives,

00:12:00.750 --> 00:12:03.530
the lack of clarity on what
people mean when they say, okay,

00:12:03.530 --> 00:12:07.500
I'm feeling depressed
versus clinical depression

00:12:07.500 --> 00:12:09.380
versus severe depression.

00:12:09.380 --> 00:12:11.650
And so how, as you both
have talked about this

00:12:11.650 --> 00:12:14.680
in public sense, how do you
usually help people understand

00:12:14.680 --> 00:12:17.703
what those phrases mean and
what the distinctions are?

00:12:18.622 --> 00:12:19.680
Actually, I guess we can start with you

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:21.130
and then maybe go to Gerry from there.

00:12:21.130 --> 00:12:22.650
- Sure, yeah.

00:12:22.650 --> 00:12:27.650
So I think there is a completion
of depression and sadness.

00:12:31.450 --> 00:12:32.780
That they are one in the same.

00:12:32.780 --> 00:12:35.200
That when I'm feeling sad or down

00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:38.010
about something that I'm
depressed, we use that term

00:12:39.580 --> 00:12:42.183
very flippantly and often,

00:12:43.520 --> 00:12:46.593
And that is not true.

00:12:51.110 --> 00:12:54.750
I also think clinical depression
can look really different

00:12:54.750 --> 00:12:57.520
for different people because you can have

00:12:57.520 --> 00:13:00.630
what I like to call like a
constellation of symptoms.

00:13:00.630 --> 00:13:03.250
And so someone's clinical depression,

00:13:03.250 --> 00:13:05.760
even if we're categorizing
as clinical depression

00:13:05.760 --> 00:13:08.060
can look really different
than someone else's.

00:13:09.020 --> 00:13:14.020
So I think it's important to talk about,

00:13:14.390 --> 00:13:17.220
and I'll just speak
mostly to my experience.

00:13:17.220 --> 00:13:18.053
And I'll let Gerry

00:13:18.053 --> 00:13:21.050
do all of, from a clinical perspective,

00:13:21.050 --> 00:13:23.640
these are kind of the lines
that we draw or what we mean

00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:24.973
or what is meaningful.

00:13:25.840 --> 00:13:29.280
But I often refer to what I'm living with

00:13:29.280 --> 00:13:30.880
is severe depression

00:13:30.880 --> 00:13:35.880
or really difficult to treat depression.

00:13:37.450 --> 00:13:41.720
And I think for me,

00:13:41.720 --> 00:13:44.530
if you think about
depression as a spectrum

00:13:44.530 --> 00:13:48.840
and you have this, oh,
things aren't feeling, right,

00:13:48.840 --> 00:13:51.610
I'm feeling down, I'm feeling fatigued.

00:13:51.610 --> 00:13:54.590
Maybe I'm not sleeping,
I'm feeling restless.

00:13:54.590 --> 00:13:56.790
And there's a whole
spectrum of where that goes

00:13:56.790 --> 00:14:00.110
to where I feel like my
depression when it's not treated

00:14:00.110 --> 00:14:02.980
or well-managed goes real fast.

00:14:02.980 --> 00:14:06.890
Which is this overwhelming fatigue

00:14:06.890 --> 00:14:11.690
that my bones feel like they
are hollow but need of iron.

00:14:11.690 --> 00:14:14.500
Like there's nothing in them.

00:14:14.500 --> 00:14:18.163
It's an empty feeling,
but it's a heavy feeling.

00:14:19.240 --> 00:14:20.930
I often feel like there's just like

00:14:20.930 --> 00:14:23.195
someone's sitting on my chest

00:14:23.195 --> 00:14:24.710
and I have to work to breathe.

00:14:24.710 --> 00:14:26.600
Not in the same way like I'm panicked

00:14:26.600 --> 00:14:27.770
and I'm struggling to breathe,

00:14:27.770 --> 00:14:29.620
but it just like, it feels laborsome.

00:14:30.920 --> 00:14:34.740
There's also, I tend to
have like a loss of affect.

00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:37.990
Of like, I'm a person, I
feel things really deeply.

00:14:37.990 --> 00:14:40.520
I'm a deep thinker in introspective.

00:14:40.520 --> 00:14:44.270
And I will know all these
things intellectually,

00:14:44.270 --> 00:14:46.190
but I will look at my
husband and I might not have

00:14:46.190 --> 00:14:47.670
any feelings toward him.

00:14:47.670 --> 00:14:52.670
That's really painful to experience.

00:14:52.880 --> 00:14:54.850
And I think for me in particular,

00:14:54.850 --> 00:14:58.250
and one thing I probably
don't do a good enough job of,

00:14:58.250 --> 00:15:01.430
saying because to me,
they go together so well,

00:15:01.430 --> 00:15:05.420
but it's not true is my
depression is often accompanied

00:15:06.372 --> 00:15:08.970
by pretty intense suicidal ideation.

00:15:08.970 --> 00:15:10.890
And that's not true for everyone.

00:15:10.890 --> 00:15:12.920
Even people who have severe depression

00:15:12.920 --> 00:15:16.800
might not have suicidal ideation.

00:15:16.800 --> 00:15:21.504
But mine tend to be pretty
close one in the same.

00:15:21.504 --> 00:15:26.504
And so, before I met Gerry

00:15:26.786 --> 00:15:30.370
and before I was in the studies
and I was walking around

00:15:30.370 --> 00:15:33.890
for four years and every single day,

00:15:33.890 --> 00:15:35.340
and it just got progressively worse.

00:15:35.340 --> 00:15:37.833
And by the time I met
Gerry, every single minute,

00:15:38.690 --> 00:15:39.810
all this I was thinking about

00:15:39.810 --> 00:15:42.400
is like how much I wanted to die.

00:15:42.400 --> 00:15:45.850
And like what a burden that is to carry

00:15:45.850 --> 00:15:48.810
and just how paralyzing that can be.

00:15:48.810 --> 00:15:51.700
And I think depending on the
constellation of symptoms

00:15:51.700 --> 00:15:53.995
I have as an adult, I
didn't when I was younger,

00:15:53.995 --> 00:15:58.500
have what I refer to as
these functional impairments.

00:15:58.500 --> 00:16:02.480
But I have trouble seeing
the future thinking past.

00:16:02.480 --> 00:16:04.390
Sometimes I can't even think past a day.

00:16:04.390 --> 00:16:07.210
Sometimes I can't even think past an hour.

00:16:07.210 --> 00:16:09.900
Which makes it really
hard to problem solve

00:16:09.900 --> 00:16:12.340
and tolerate and do anything.

00:16:12.340 --> 00:16:15.000
So I think there's a range there,

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:18.383
but for me personally, my
experience, that's what I mean.

00:16:19.660 --> 00:16:21.420
- Yeah, I think that's
really helpful just the way

00:16:21.420 --> 00:16:23.344
that you've painted that picture

00:16:23.344 --> 00:16:24.630
and even the bravery
in sharing that as well

00:16:24.630 --> 00:16:27.000
because I'm imagining people

00:16:27.000 --> 00:16:28.520
who've walked through that themselves,

00:16:28.520 --> 00:16:30.380
hearing someone else talk about it,

00:16:30.380 --> 00:16:32.310
how helpful and validating that could be.

00:16:32.310 --> 00:16:33.930
And for those who have
friends and loved ones

00:16:33.930 --> 00:16:38.240
who don't always know how to
respond or how to react to it,

00:16:38.240 --> 00:16:39.990
just hearing that personal perspective,

00:16:39.990 --> 00:16:43.440
I think is so helpful in terms of context.

00:16:43.440 --> 00:16:46.250
Really appreciate the way
that you've painted that.

00:16:46.250 --> 00:16:48.480
And also just clarified some
of the distinctions as well.

00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:50.780
'Cause you're right, we do
use it, not intentionally,

00:16:50.780 --> 00:16:53.670
but it really is important
to have that distinction

00:16:53.670 --> 00:16:57.230
so people can really understand
the differences as well.

00:16:57.230 --> 00:16:58.150
Gerry, anything you want--
- I think--

00:16:58.150 --> 00:16:59.497
- Oh, go ahead, Ashley.

00:16:59.497 --> 00:17:00.330
- I was just gonna say the one thing

00:17:00.330 --> 00:17:04.530
that I also think about is we
also didn't think of happiness

00:17:04.530 --> 00:17:07.017
as the opposite of depression.

00:17:07.017 --> 00:17:09.480
And happiness might, I'm not convinced,

00:17:09.480 --> 00:17:12.630
but might be the opposite
sadness, which is not depression.

00:17:12.630 --> 00:17:13.607
Andrew Solomon said it.

00:17:13.607 --> 00:17:14.587
And he was like,

00:17:14.587 --> 00:17:17.290
"The opposite of
depression isn't happiness.

00:17:17.290 --> 00:17:20.250
The opposite of depression is vitality."

00:17:20.250 --> 00:17:25.130
I feel like that, like
yes, those things feel.

00:17:25.130 --> 00:17:27.439
- Yeah, that's really good.

00:17:27.439 --> 00:17:29.690
That reminds me of what
you said the onset too,

00:17:29.690 --> 00:17:32.660
just that you can be okay and
now okay at the same time.

00:17:32.660 --> 00:17:36.410
And that that can be a good place to be.

00:17:36.410 --> 00:17:37.770
It can also be a challenging place to be.

00:17:37.770 --> 00:17:40.740
And there's not one
static response to that,

00:17:40.740 --> 00:17:42.930
but even that in itself doesn't convey

00:17:43.820 --> 00:17:45.810
a sense of happiness all the time.

00:17:45.810 --> 00:17:47.000
Which I think sometimes like you said,

00:17:47.000 --> 00:17:47.850
people are looking for,

00:17:47.850 --> 00:17:51.620
but that's just not the reality
of successfully navigating

00:17:51.620 --> 00:17:52.550
mental health in general.

00:17:52.550 --> 00:17:54.250
So really appreciate that as well.

00:17:56.210 --> 00:17:57.690
Gerry, anything that you wanna add there,

00:17:57.690 --> 00:17:59.062
even in terms of a big picture,

00:17:59.062 --> 00:18:00.940
painting that picture for us?

00:18:00.940 --> 00:18:03.160
- Yeah, actually, I
pick up on a few things

00:18:03.160 --> 00:18:05.282
that Ashley said, but I
think psychiatry in general

00:18:05.282 --> 00:18:09.100
is really plagued by having a lexicon

00:18:09.100 --> 00:18:12.750
that overlaps so much with lay language.

00:18:12.750 --> 00:18:15.610
And these words like
feeling depressed and said

00:18:17.124 --> 00:18:21.210
are get used interchangeably
where they really aren't.

00:18:21.210 --> 00:18:23.773
Sadness is a symptom of depression.

00:18:24.610 --> 00:18:27.223
And we use a lot of words in psychiatry,

00:18:28.220 --> 00:18:31.110
in our common conversations

00:18:31.110 --> 00:18:34.270
that we have a different clinical meaning

00:18:34.270 --> 00:18:36.483
when we're really being specific about it.

00:18:37.440 --> 00:18:40.610
But I think the other issue
that Ashley touched upon,

00:18:40.610 --> 00:18:43.940
which is so critical
is depression is truly,

00:18:43.940 --> 00:18:48.850
it's what we call a
wastepaper basket diagnosis.

00:18:48.850 --> 00:18:52.200
Meaning it's a diagnosis of exclusion.

00:18:52.200 --> 00:18:56.560
So you have these set
of symptoms and signs

00:18:56.560 --> 00:18:58.890
and we can't find a reason for them.

00:18:58.890 --> 00:19:01.844
If we find a reason for them,
you don't have depression.

00:19:01.844 --> 00:19:04.380
If you have hypothyroidism,
you have hypothyroidism.

00:19:04.380 --> 00:19:08.117
If you're early stage
Parkinson's, you have Parkinson's.

00:19:08.117 --> 00:19:10.180
So depression by definition is something

00:19:10.180 --> 00:19:12.490
that we can't really identify.

00:19:12.490 --> 00:19:14.560
We don't have a positive
test for depression.

00:19:14.560 --> 00:19:17.960
We have symptoms and we rule
out all the other alternatives.

00:19:17.960 --> 00:19:19.820
And if you can't find anything,

00:19:19.820 --> 00:19:22.490
we're left with the
diagnosis of depression.

00:19:22.490 --> 00:19:25.510
And that alone, as you could imagine,

00:19:25.510 --> 00:19:27.823
makes it a very heterogeneous disorder.

00:19:28.990 --> 00:19:31.370
I mean, there are probably
many different ways

00:19:31.370 --> 00:19:33.300
you can get to that end point

00:19:33.300 --> 00:19:34.840
of having those signs and symptoms.

00:19:34.840 --> 00:19:36.850
So it makes it very difficult then.

00:19:36.850 --> 00:19:39.460
And again, following with
what Ashley was saying,

00:19:39.460 --> 00:19:42.150
it isn't a sort of dichotomous thing

00:19:42.150 --> 00:19:43.670
where you're depressed or not depressed.

00:19:43.670 --> 00:19:47.140
In fact, most people don't fit that thing.

00:19:47.140 --> 00:19:49.853
It's on a continuum of how far.

00:19:51.290 --> 00:19:53.110
And not only just on a continuum,

00:19:53.110 --> 00:19:55.620
it's probably not a single axis.

00:19:55.620 --> 00:19:59.120
So it probably is not happy to sad.

00:19:59.120 --> 00:20:03.190
In fact, neurobiologically,
it's not same brain regions

00:20:03.190 --> 00:20:05.140
that control things like reward

00:20:05.140 --> 00:20:06.930
and control things like anxiety.

00:20:06.930 --> 00:20:09.620
There's actually different
brain regions that are involved.

00:20:09.620 --> 00:20:11.618
And there's no reason to think

00:20:11.618 --> 00:20:14.940
that this is on a slide
rule that you just go

00:20:14.940 --> 00:20:15.840
from one to the other,

00:20:15.840 --> 00:20:18.390
there's probably multiple
different axes involved

00:20:18.390 --> 00:20:19.410
in the way you're feeling.

00:20:19.410 --> 00:20:21.630
So very long winded way of saying

00:20:21.630 --> 00:20:23.810
it's complicated and complex.

00:20:23.810 --> 00:20:25.450
I really, for me, it boils down

00:20:25.450 --> 00:20:29.760
to the functional impact
on somebody's life

00:20:29.760 --> 00:20:34.000
and the quality of life and
those two big issues determining

00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:36.230
whether it needs to be treated.

00:20:36.230 --> 00:20:38.384
How it needs to be treated,

00:20:38.384 --> 00:20:42.393
it is a very individualistic
type model to approach.

00:20:43.580 --> 00:20:46.530
- Yeah, but I definitely
appreciate that context as well.

00:20:46.530 --> 00:20:49.040
And especially functionality is I think,

00:20:49.040 --> 00:20:51.080
for many of us who are
mental health advocates,

00:20:51.080 --> 00:20:52.630
that's something we often talk about.

00:20:52.630 --> 00:20:54.580
Whether people notice a significant change

00:20:54.580 --> 00:20:57.690
that's impairing day to day functioning.

00:20:57.690 --> 00:20:58.680
Our day-to-day activities,

00:20:58.680 --> 00:21:02.240
there's a big shift from
people's normal interactions

00:21:02.240 --> 00:21:03.073
in the world.

00:21:03.073 --> 00:21:04.310
But then I was also
curious what you mentioned

00:21:04.310 --> 00:21:06.140
about the exclusion piece

00:21:06.140 --> 00:21:07.730
and for either of you, I'm curious.

00:21:07.730 --> 00:21:12.170
Does that ever become a point
of frustration for patients?

00:21:12.170 --> 00:21:13.640
I can imagine somebody saying,

00:21:13.640 --> 00:21:15.490
well, you're just ruling out
everything else and saying,

00:21:15.490 --> 00:21:17.160
so it must be depression.

00:21:17.160 --> 00:21:19.910
And that, as a patient,

00:21:19.910 --> 00:21:22.830
I can imagine that would feel
very frustrating in itself.

00:21:22.830 --> 00:21:23.860
Are you sure it is?

00:21:23.860 --> 00:21:27.590
Or what's underlying, and just
kind of a feeling of unknown?

00:21:27.590 --> 00:21:30.714
Well, it's just this, because
we can't find anything else.

00:21:30.714 --> 00:21:32.800
How do those conversations?

00:21:32.800 --> 00:21:35.030
- I promise you as a
clinician and as a scientist,

00:21:35.030 --> 00:21:36.330
it's very frustrating too.

00:21:37.460 --> 00:21:41.850
I think it's humbling.

00:21:41.850 --> 00:21:46.430
And I approach this by saying,

00:21:46.430 --> 00:21:48.490
I let people know that
just because we don't know

00:21:48.490 --> 00:21:50.710
exactly what it is
doesn't mean we don't know

00:21:50.710 --> 00:21:52.520
how to provide treatment.

00:21:52.520 --> 00:21:55.160
We don't know how to make things better.

00:21:55.160 --> 00:21:59.510
We can know that without knowing exactly

00:21:59.510 --> 00:22:01.440
what's going on neurobiologically

00:22:01.440 --> 00:22:03.823
or having the perfect diagnosis.

00:22:04.699 --> 00:22:06.260
To take it to the extreme.

00:22:06.260 --> 00:22:09.540
I always remind people that Newton

00:22:09.540 --> 00:22:12.160
isn't completely right
in classical physics,

00:22:12.160 --> 00:22:13.790
but it's still enough way can build

00:22:13.790 --> 00:22:14.740
the Empire State Building

00:22:14.740 --> 00:22:16.450
and have it standing
for hundreds of years.

00:22:16.450 --> 00:22:20.330
So we can have enough of an understanding

00:22:20.330 --> 00:22:22.400
to understand how to treat these,

00:22:22.400 --> 00:22:24.710
but we really don't know

00:22:24.710 --> 00:22:26.953
the underlying neurobiology
for depression.

00:22:26.953 --> 00:22:29.890
It's something I know
you're working hard at,

00:22:29.890 --> 00:22:31.980
our lab is working hard at.

00:22:31.980 --> 00:22:34.230
I think we're getting closer year by year,

00:22:34.230 --> 00:22:36.383
but we're not there yet.

00:22:39.140 --> 00:22:41.360
- Ashley, anything that you
wanted to add on that point?

00:22:41.360 --> 00:22:46.100
Both either as an activist
or as someone walking through

00:22:46.100 --> 00:22:48.620
and navigating mental
illness over the years.

00:22:48.620 --> 00:22:49.453
- Yeah, yeah.

00:22:50.306 --> 00:22:52.940
So, I would say sounds terrible,

00:22:52.940 --> 00:22:55.980
but you're lucky if you find
a doctor who's also gonna look

00:22:55.980 --> 00:22:58.350
for other things and not
assume it's depression.

00:22:58.350 --> 00:23:00.410
Where the might be, the rule-out of,

00:23:00.410 --> 00:23:01.870
we looked for all these other things,

00:23:01.870 --> 00:23:04.170
but I think depending on what kind of care

00:23:04.170 --> 00:23:05.003
you have access to,

00:23:05.003 --> 00:23:06.740
and maybe even where
you live in the country

00:23:06.740 --> 00:23:09.993
and maybe who that first
doctor is that you encounter.

00:23:10.990 --> 00:23:14.793
They may or may not look for other things.

00:23:16.080 --> 00:23:21.080
And I've struggled with
some form of mental illness

00:23:22.930 --> 00:23:25.880
pretty much since I was in middle,

00:23:25.880 --> 00:23:28.380
I would say at least middle
school, if not sooner.

00:23:30.020 --> 00:23:34.220
I had a pretty traumatic
childhood and had a lot

00:23:34.220 --> 00:23:38.040
of symptoms of PTSD

00:23:38.040 --> 00:23:42.210
and started self-harming
when I was very young.

00:23:42.210 --> 00:23:46.270
And so I didn't have the same,

00:23:46.270 --> 00:23:49.440
I don't have the same intense story

00:23:49.440 --> 00:23:52.410
as some people who might
become depressed as an adult,

00:23:52.410 --> 00:23:57.003
or might have their first
onset or episode later.

00:23:58.070 --> 00:24:00.540
It was pretty smack in my face.

00:24:00.540 --> 00:24:03.460
Like something's really terribly wrong.

00:24:03.460 --> 00:24:08.103
And so I was connected with
care at a pretty young age.

00:24:11.804 --> 00:24:14.500
And I've been fortunate that
I've had continued access

00:24:14.500 --> 00:24:17.810
to care that my parents
had good health insurance,

00:24:17.810 --> 00:24:19.860
that I was able to go to good hospitals.

00:24:19.860 --> 00:24:22.053
I was in and out of hospitals.

00:24:23.530 --> 00:24:28.097
And had pretty good team for awhile.

00:24:30.740 --> 00:24:34.720
And I would say, definitely
depression there,

00:24:34.720 --> 00:24:36.590
but they're all arbitrary lines,

00:24:36.590 --> 00:24:40.610
but it was mostly really
kind of post-traumatic stress

00:24:40.610 --> 00:24:41.970
and in reactions to being

00:24:41.970 --> 00:24:46.970
in some pretty not great situations.

00:24:47.000 --> 00:24:48.530
And I've been really fortunate

00:24:48.530 --> 00:24:50.760
that I've had a lot of therapy for that

00:24:50.760 --> 00:24:52.760
and some time has passed where

00:24:53.664 --> 00:24:58.664
that although, it's still
there and still comes up

00:24:59.280 --> 00:25:00.690
and bubbles up from time to time

00:25:00.690 --> 00:25:02.950
really isn't something
that kind of interferes

00:25:02.950 --> 00:25:05.570
with my daily life in a way.

00:25:05.570 --> 00:25:10.290
But what as an adult, as that subsided,

00:25:10.290 --> 00:25:13.860
I think, there was a process
where I think depression

00:25:13.860 --> 00:25:15.890
really set in and took over

00:25:15.890 --> 00:25:18.430
and has been the thing that I think

00:25:18.430 --> 00:25:23.430
has been a continuous struggle
for me for the last 10 years.

00:25:25.090 --> 00:25:27.763
So for me, it was a little clearer.

00:25:28.930 --> 00:25:31.770
I do also have, I have
an autoimmune disorder

00:25:34.776 --> 00:25:36.240
and I think we have just discovered

00:25:36.240 --> 00:25:38.610
that I have a primary immune deficiency.

00:25:38.610 --> 00:25:41.530
And I know you had a guest
who was talking about that.

00:25:41.530 --> 00:25:43.680
He has a much more severe case than I do.

00:25:43.680 --> 00:25:47.443
But as a patient who fatigue,

00:25:48.810 --> 00:25:50.830
it's something I really struggle with,

00:25:50.830 --> 00:25:52.320
and it's a symptom of depression

00:25:52.320 --> 00:25:55.319
and it's a symptom of
an autoimmune disorder.

00:25:55.319 --> 00:25:58.290
And then just in the last three months,

00:25:58.290 --> 00:26:02.170
have found out, oh, I have
these other deficiencies that,

00:26:02.170 --> 00:26:04.370
when those are fixed, maybe
I'll have some energy.

00:26:04.370 --> 00:26:07.590
So I also get the frustration
of my psychiatrist

00:26:07.590 --> 00:26:09.330
being like, it's your
auto immune disorder.

00:26:09.330 --> 00:26:12.360
And my rheumatologists being
like, it's your depression.

00:26:12.360 --> 00:26:13.950
And we're all just, and I'm like,

00:26:13.950 --> 00:26:16.173
I don't care but someone, please fix it.

00:26:19.000 --> 00:26:23.393
So I appreciate that part too, so.

00:26:24.594 --> 00:26:26.269
- And I do.

00:26:26.269 --> 00:26:28.473
- Yeah, I do, I just wanna make sure

00:26:28.473 --> 00:26:30.340
that that the point I made previously

00:26:30.340 --> 00:26:32.883
and following up on what
Ashley said is clear.

00:26:35.060 --> 00:26:36.620
I mean, I think we need to be honest

00:26:36.620 --> 00:26:39.740
and be clear that we don't know

00:26:39.740 --> 00:26:41.950
the real underlying neurobiology.

00:26:41.950 --> 00:26:46.000
But that shouldn't be taken to mean

00:26:46.000 --> 00:26:48.224
that we don't know what to do.

00:26:48.224 --> 00:26:50.176
We have to treat and help people.

00:26:50.176 --> 00:26:54.210
And is actually saying what we call it

00:26:54.210 --> 00:26:56.570
is actually less important than knowing

00:26:56.570 --> 00:26:58.580
how to have treatments that are effective.

00:26:58.580 --> 00:26:59.520
And Ashley's right.

00:26:59.520 --> 00:27:01.880
We're right on the cusp
of understanding the role

00:27:01.880 --> 00:27:05.230
that neuroinflammation or
neuroinflammatory disease

00:27:05.230 --> 00:27:08.910
could have or immune
deficiency in these disorders.

00:27:08.910 --> 00:27:10.990
And so we don't really know the cause.

00:27:10.990 --> 00:27:13.373
And hopefully someday, we
can say nobody has depression

00:27:13.373 --> 00:27:16.860
because we can identify
what it is, then treat it.

00:27:16.860 --> 00:27:19.760
But as it is, we're pretty good

00:27:19.760 --> 00:27:24.760
about treating the general
diagnosis as it stands today.

00:27:25.616 --> 00:27:27.397
It could be better and
we're getting better,

00:27:27.397 --> 00:27:29.210
but as it is, we're pretty good.

00:27:29.210 --> 00:27:30.043
- Yeah, yeah.

00:27:30.043 --> 00:27:32.540
And that's definitely well-taken as well.

00:27:32.540 --> 00:27:36.530
And even, not always knowing the reason,

00:27:36.530 --> 00:27:38.140
but at least being able to move forward

00:27:38.140 --> 00:27:40.860
in the treatment phase as well.

00:27:40.860 --> 00:27:42.440
On that note, I also
wanna pick up on something

00:27:42.440 --> 00:27:44.003
that Ashley mentioned as well

00:27:44.003 --> 00:27:46.610
just in terms of having providers

00:27:46.610 --> 00:27:50.470
who were in that mindset
and trying to make sure

00:27:50.470 --> 00:27:54.090
that they treated the depression.

00:27:54.090 --> 00:27:54.923
And this in a sense,

00:27:54.923 --> 00:27:57.080
is a little bit bigger than
the depression topic itself.

00:27:57.080 --> 00:27:58.260
And just mental health in general,

00:27:58.260 --> 00:28:00.410
as we've also talked
about on this podcast,

00:28:00.410 --> 00:28:02.600
just not only access to care,

00:28:02.600 --> 00:28:06.500
but how providers react to
people from different backgrounds

00:28:06.500 --> 00:28:10.100
and the aspects of how race
plays in, racism or zip code,

00:28:10.100 --> 00:28:14.680
and whether that is uniformly
accessible across the board.

00:28:14.680 --> 00:28:16.530
But even say, getting past that level

00:28:16.530 --> 00:28:18.880
of saying that is accessible,
let's make that assumption,

00:28:18.880 --> 00:28:21.040
even though we know that's
not true universally.

00:28:21.040 --> 00:28:23.238
But even once that is provided, Ashley,

00:28:23.238 --> 00:28:27.647
you've talked a lot about just
navigating the system as well

00:28:27.647 --> 00:28:31.380
and the ups and downs that
you had in that process.

00:28:31.380 --> 00:28:33.130
So thinking about your 2019 piece,

00:28:33.130 --> 00:28:36.540
my struggle to access
lifesaving mental health care,

00:28:36.540 --> 00:28:38.590
I was wondering if you could
actually just take us through,

00:28:38.590 --> 00:28:39.910
as we move to that topic,

00:28:39.910 --> 00:28:43.020
how you went about putting
that piece together.

00:28:43.020 --> 00:28:45.313
What was that process like for you?

00:28:46.480 --> 00:28:47.570
- Sure, yeah.

00:28:47.570 --> 00:28:49.520
Of writing the article or?

00:28:49.520 --> 00:28:50.353
- Yeah.

00:28:51.360 --> 00:28:53.760
What motivated you to
actually put it out there?

00:28:53.760 --> 00:28:54.593
- Yeah, sure.

00:28:56.205 --> 00:28:58.200
I like to get people up to speed

00:28:58.200 --> 00:28:59.550
if you haven't read the piece.

00:28:59.550 --> 00:29:01.280
But like I said, I met Gerry.

00:29:01.280 --> 00:29:03.840
I was in some clinical
trials 'cause unfortunately,

00:29:03.840 --> 00:29:07.290
I was one of those people
who we didn't know really

00:29:07.290 --> 00:29:09.256
how to treat my depression.

00:29:09.256 --> 00:29:13.053
I had a very good doctor and
I'm what I like to consider

00:29:13.053 --> 00:29:14.270
a very good patient.

00:29:14.270 --> 00:29:15.350
I'm willing to try things.

00:29:15.350 --> 00:29:16.650
And if I'm not, I'm gonna tell you

00:29:16.650 --> 00:29:17.997
and we'll get a better plan,

00:29:17.997 --> 00:29:20.800
but I'm willing to hang in there and see.

00:29:20.800 --> 00:29:22.500
And so, I mean, we had been trying

00:29:22.500 --> 00:29:24.450
for at this point, two years,

00:29:24.450 --> 00:29:27.080
different combination of some medicines,

00:29:27.080 --> 00:29:30.140
different classes of
medicines, all sorts of things.

00:29:30.140 --> 00:29:32.790
I was running, I was doing yoga.

00:29:32.790 --> 00:29:35.360
I was behaviorally doing
everything I should be doing.

00:29:35.360 --> 00:29:36.270
I was in therapy.

00:29:36.270 --> 00:29:38.310
I switched to another kind of therapy.

00:29:38.310 --> 00:29:40.712
And I was getting worse.

00:29:40.712 --> 00:29:44.220
So by the time I found Gerry,

00:29:44.220 --> 00:29:48.027
I think everyone was just
like, I don't know what to do.

00:29:48.027 --> 00:29:51.020
And I was lucky that I did
respond really well to ketamine.

00:29:51.020 --> 00:29:53.050
And we can talk about that later.

00:29:53.050 --> 00:29:55.490
Which is what I received
in the clinical trial.

00:29:55.490 --> 00:30:00.490
But in that process of
enrolling in a clinical trial

00:30:01.760 --> 00:30:03.260
and you get access to this care

00:30:03.260 --> 00:30:05.010
that you can't get otherwise.

00:30:05.010 --> 00:30:07.950
It's still being studied and ketamine

00:30:07.950 --> 00:30:10.880
because it is FDA approved
for other reasons,

00:30:10.880 --> 00:30:13.776
you can access it unlike
if it's experimental drug

00:30:13.776 --> 00:30:16.713
without any kind of approval.

00:30:18.610 --> 00:30:23.090
But then the question always
is like, what do you do after?

00:30:23.090 --> 00:30:24.690
What do you do after the study ends?

00:30:24.690 --> 00:30:26.920
What do you do after the protocol ends?

00:30:26.920 --> 00:30:28.060
Protocols are different.

00:30:28.060 --> 00:30:29.420
Sometimes, you have continued access.

00:30:29.420 --> 00:30:31.780
Sometimes it's a one and done thing.

00:30:31.780 --> 00:30:35.740
And so, we were in that
situation of, oh my gosh,

00:30:35.740 --> 00:30:36.670
I found something.

00:30:36.670 --> 00:30:38.623
I responded super well to it.

00:30:39.480 --> 00:30:41.560
I can't get access to it otherwise.

00:30:41.560 --> 00:30:43.110
My insurance isn't gonna pay for it.

00:30:43.110 --> 00:30:44.820
It's very, very expensive.

00:30:44.820 --> 00:30:47.550
There are very few
people, who at that time,

00:30:47.550 --> 00:30:49.190
were receiving it.

00:30:49.190 --> 00:30:51.903
Ketamine on an off label outpatient basis.

00:30:52.840 --> 00:30:57.840
And I ended up in the
hospital for over a month

00:30:59.440 --> 00:31:01.580
on full medical leave.

00:31:01.580 --> 00:31:05.540
I tried electroconvulsive therapy

00:31:05.540 --> 00:31:08.350
because that is the gold standard

00:31:08.350 --> 00:31:10.500
and that was the best option.

00:31:10.500 --> 00:31:12.623
We had really exhausted options.

00:31:14.256 --> 00:31:17.480
And unfortunately, I just didn't
respond to that treatment.

00:31:17.480 --> 00:31:20.430
And so after that process,

00:31:20.430 --> 00:31:23.182
Gerry and some other physicians
at Yale New Haven Hospital

00:31:23.182 --> 00:31:25.610
were able to get me into the program

00:31:25.610 --> 00:31:29.350
to be able to receive ketamine
outside of a study setting,

00:31:29.350 --> 00:31:30.600
really to save my life.

00:31:30.600 --> 00:31:35.020
But it was, I mean, I'll
let Gerry speak to it too.

00:31:35.020 --> 00:31:38.360
I mean, it was a terrible,
terrible struggle

00:31:38.360 --> 00:31:43.360
where it was treatment
by treatment approval

00:31:43.620 --> 00:31:45.430
by whoever the administrators are.

00:31:45.430 --> 00:31:49.270
And love you, thank you for
doing that and saving my life.

00:31:49.270 --> 00:31:53.054
But really, it was somewhat
out of the doctor's control

00:31:53.054 --> 00:31:55.873
or quite out of the doctor's control.

00:31:57.277 --> 00:31:59.790
And it was particularly frustrating

00:31:59.790 --> 00:32:01.620
'cause I knew there was
something that worked.

00:32:01.620 --> 00:32:03.220
It wasn't like, I didn't have any hope.

00:32:03.220 --> 00:32:07.083
Like I knew I was like, if you
can get me this, I will live.

00:32:09.510 --> 00:32:14.510
And I think being that
unwell and having to advocate

00:32:16.010 --> 00:32:18.860
for treatment and it was exhausting.

00:32:18.860 --> 00:32:21.870
The tiny resources I had left
that I could have been using

00:32:21.870 --> 00:32:25.850
to try to replenish
some internal resources

00:32:25.850 --> 00:32:30.580
to make the case or connect
with the right person

00:32:33.049 --> 00:32:37.610
or I mean, I feel like I
was willing to beg to do,

00:32:37.610 --> 00:32:41.023
I will do anything
because I'm going to die.

00:32:42.130 --> 00:32:45.103
And it was terrifying.

00:32:47.070 --> 00:32:50.660
And I was so lucky.

00:32:50.660 --> 00:32:54.830
And so I think once I got
approval to receive this treatment

00:32:54.830 --> 00:32:58.590
for free, like on that day,

00:32:58.590 --> 00:32:59.910
look, if it hadn't happened that day,

00:32:59.910 --> 00:33:02.900
I'd had a conversation with one
of my doctors and just said,

00:33:02.900 --> 00:33:07.020
like, I don't think you guys
understand how unwell I am.

00:33:07.020 --> 00:33:08.323
And it's gonna be too late.

00:33:08.323 --> 00:33:12.480
If we don't fix this,
it's gonna be too late.

00:33:12.480 --> 00:33:15.930
And had I walked out that
day with a different answer,

00:33:15.930 --> 00:33:18.430
it certainly would have been too late.

00:33:18.430 --> 00:33:20.143
I mean, that's where I was.

00:33:21.440 --> 00:33:24.710
And I think, I quickly felt well

00:33:24.710 --> 00:33:26.000
after about a month of treatment

00:33:26.000 --> 00:33:29.187
which was such a blessing
from being so unwell. But

00:33:29.187 --> 00:33:34.187
But I knew that like luck
played a huge role in that.

00:33:34.577 --> 00:33:37.810
And that made me very, very unsettled

00:33:37.810 --> 00:33:39.420
and scared as a person.

00:33:39.420 --> 00:33:42.320
And I also recognized
like of this situation,

00:33:42.320 --> 00:33:46.770
I am better situated than 99.5% of people

00:33:46.770 --> 00:33:49.900
to have negotiated this
and gotten through it.

00:33:49.900 --> 00:33:52.290
I mean, I'm white, I'm well educated.

00:33:52.290 --> 00:33:53.820
I'm very well connected.

00:33:53.820 --> 00:33:56.330
I worked in the Department of Psychiatry.

00:33:56.330 --> 00:34:00.020
Half of my friends are
psychiatrist or psychologist.

00:34:00.020 --> 00:34:04.130
I mean, I had been in the
system for a long time.

00:34:04.130 --> 00:34:06.423
So I know about the healthcare system.

00:34:07.410 --> 00:34:08.930
I research it.

00:34:08.930 --> 00:34:13.930
I mean, I just was so
well-equipped and I almost died.

00:34:15.730 --> 00:34:18.674
And I was like, oh, this is what happens.

00:34:18.674 --> 00:34:22.910
This is how people fall
through the cracks.

00:34:22.910 --> 00:34:25.050
And this is how people
die because not everyone

00:34:25.050 --> 00:34:28.070
is as well resourced
and connected as I am.

00:34:28.070 --> 00:34:31.570
And so that was what
motivated it part and part.

00:34:31.570 --> 00:34:33.350
And part of it, it was selfish.

00:34:33.350 --> 00:34:35.760
I was terrified it was gonna happen again.

00:34:35.760 --> 00:34:38.600
And I needed to have a
better system because I knew

00:34:38.600 --> 00:34:42.050
that I will not get lucky twice.

00:34:42.050 --> 00:34:43.423
And I also knew, like,

00:34:45.320 --> 00:34:49.190
I wouldn't say if I had to do
it again, I wouldn't survive.

00:34:49.190 --> 00:34:52.390
I still kind of believe that of like,

00:34:52.390 --> 00:34:53.990
it is remarkable that I survived

00:34:53.990 --> 00:34:55.043
and I'm so thankful for that.

00:34:55.043 --> 00:34:59.141
But if I had to do it again,
I'm still not sure I would.

00:34:59.141 --> 00:35:02.920
And so I wrote this piece in
part for me to have like, yes,

00:35:02.920 --> 00:35:05.760
to help others and build a
better system and try to think

00:35:05.760 --> 00:35:06.820
about things differently.

00:35:06.820 --> 00:35:10.220
But then also selfishly, I was like,

00:35:10.220 --> 00:35:12.337
I almost died and I don't
want that to happen again.

00:35:12.337 --> 00:35:13.828
- Yeah.

00:35:13.828 --> 00:35:16.260
I'm grateful that you're here.

00:35:16.260 --> 00:35:19.710
Grateful that you took
the time to write that

00:35:19.710 --> 00:35:21.090
on so many levels.

00:35:21.090 --> 00:35:24.580
But at the same time,
it's so sobering as well.

00:35:24.580 --> 00:35:27.830
Just to hear how close that was,

00:35:27.830 --> 00:35:31.020
even with all the resources
that you had access to.

00:35:31.020 --> 00:35:33.170
And I say that not in the
sense that you had access

00:35:33.170 --> 00:35:34.770
to everything you needed,
but comparatively,

00:35:34.770 --> 00:35:37.580
as you mentioned, to others who not have

00:35:37.580 --> 00:35:39.683
those same resources and even still.

00:35:41.580 --> 00:35:46.216
- And well, resources, but
also like the best doctors.

00:35:46.216 --> 00:35:49.360
The people who are at
the top of the field,

00:35:49.360 --> 00:35:50.470
cutting edge, everything.

00:35:50.470 --> 00:35:53.173
And then also who are just
like very good humans.

00:35:54.620 --> 00:35:59.130
So it really was like the
perfect situation to be in,

00:35:59.130 --> 00:36:02.110
except for, it was a whole hot mess

00:36:02.110 --> 00:36:07.110
because the system is very
broken, if there is a system.

00:36:11.830 --> 00:36:13.526
- Yeah, and Gerry, go ahead.

00:36:13.526 --> 00:36:15.287
And I just wanna hear
your your perspective too

00:36:15.287 --> 00:36:17.500
and your reactions in those moments.

00:36:17.500 --> 00:36:19.060
And even as you and Ashley have continued

00:36:19.060 --> 00:36:20.100
to talk through this--

00:36:20.100 --> 00:36:25.100
- So there is the aspect
where things the system,

00:36:26.230 --> 00:36:28.230
I don't even know if it's fair
to say the system is broken

00:36:28.230 --> 00:36:29.796
and I'm not quite sure

00:36:29.796 --> 00:36:32.720
it was ever put together
properly to be broken.

00:36:32.720 --> 00:36:36.750
But obviously, the system
is a tremendous challenge.

00:36:36.750 --> 00:36:40.040
And especially in getting
behavioral health care

00:36:40.040 --> 00:36:41.850
or mental health care.

00:36:41.850 --> 00:36:43.596
But the positive side,
actually sort of this

00:36:43.596 --> 00:36:48.596
was seeing how people, when
you put them in a room,

00:36:48.970 --> 00:36:51.760
including the physicians that
really worked hard for this,

00:36:51.760 --> 00:36:53.450
but the hospital administrators,

00:36:53.450 --> 00:36:55.540
we were very fortunate to
be at a university hospital

00:36:55.540 --> 00:36:57.920
where hospital administrators
really worked hard

00:36:57.920 --> 00:36:59.100
to make this happen.

00:36:59.100 --> 00:37:02.020
But even the third-party
payers, the insurance companies.

00:37:02.020 --> 00:37:04.313
When we got to talk to
the people from there,

00:37:06.270 --> 00:37:09.473
there are real people behind
that want to help people.

00:37:10.600 --> 00:37:15.180
So I think I was very
encouraged by the fact

00:37:15.180 --> 00:37:19.970
that individuals want to
make the system better.

00:37:19.970 --> 00:37:21.837
But the system, again,

00:37:22.839 --> 00:37:24.760
I'm not even sure I can
say needs a major overhaul

00:37:24.760 --> 00:37:26.660
because I'm never quite
sure it was put together,

00:37:26.660 --> 00:37:28.060
the work made to begin with.

00:37:28.900 --> 00:37:32.900
But clearly the mental health
system in this country,

00:37:32.900 --> 00:37:37.640
but throughout the world is
not prepared for the need

00:37:37.640 --> 00:37:39.283
that's placed on it right now.

00:37:40.580 --> 00:37:45.580
It's a tremendous burden and
the cost of it is substantial.

00:37:45.680 --> 00:37:49.840
And we have to figure out a way
of delivering all treatments

00:37:49.840 --> 00:37:52.730
more efficiently and effectively,

00:37:52.730 --> 00:37:55.693
or else, we just can't afford to do this.

00:37:57.580 --> 00:37:59.380
- That's very so, so true.

00:37:59.380 --> 00:38:02.400
And again, I mean, just the weight of it

00:38:02.400 --> 00:38:06.490
and the impact and the sobering
nature as you both share,

00:38:06.490 --> 00:38:08.270
but actually, especially as
you've shared your story,

00:38:08.270 --> 00:38:11.227
and I know you shared some
suggestions in the piece as well,

00:38:11.227 --> 00:38:15.170
but I'm curious how, I
mean, that came out in 2019.

00:38:15.170 --> 00:38:16.840
Have these conversations moved in?

00:38:16.840 --> 00:38:20.150
How have you all, both in
your individual spheres

00:38:20.150 --> 00:38:24.500
and even collectively tried
to move things forward to,

00:38:24.500 --> 00:38:27.740
I don't know the right word
if it's not fixing the system,

00:38:27.740 --> 00:38:31.020
but to get to a place that's better.

00:38:31.020 --> 00:38:32.410
'Cause I mean, Ashley, I agree with that.

00:38:32.410 --> 00:38:34.600
I mean, it's a matter of life and death.

00:38:34.600 --> 00:38:36.360
There's a lot of situations
to be completely frank.

00:38:36.360 --> 00:38:39.560
And how do we ensure and move forward

00:38:39.560 --> 00:38:41.883
to more likelihood of life?

00:38:43.270 --> 00:38:47.730
- Yeah, so, I'm really thankful

00:38:47.730 --> 00:38:49.290
for the relationship I have with Gerry

00:38:49.290 --> 00:38:54.180
in part because I get to be
in conversations like this.

00:38:54.180 --> 00:38:56.430
Where I get to share my story

00:38:58.390 --> 00:39:01.740
both to help those who are suffering

00:39:01.740 --> 00:39:05.870
feel like they're not
alone and a path forward,

00:39:05.870 --> 00:39:10.870
but also I think to speak to
the gravity of the situation.

00:39:11.940 --> 00:39:16.680
And I don't think we, as a
society, appreciate that.

00:39:16.680 --> 00:39:21.680
Of how life threatening depression can be

00:39:22.835 --> 00:39:27.183
and how immediately urgent
and emergent it can be.

00:39:28.360 --> 00:39:30.230
And we definitely don't fund it

00:39:30.230 --> 00:39:32.270
the way we fund other illnesses

00:39:32.270 --> 00:39:36.680
of similar severity or threat.

00:39:36.680 --> 00:39:40.340
It was interesting in this process in 2016

00:39:40.340 --> 00:39:43.020
when I was on my first medical leave.

00:39:43.020 --> 00:39:45.520
My brother was on his medical leave

00:39:45.520 --> 00:39:47.470
after having brain surgery from,

00:39:47.470 --> 00:39:48.930
that was, he had cancer

00:39:48.930 --> 00:39:52.130
and we would talk about how
different our experiences were

00:39:53.858 --> 00:39:57.540
and how both socially and who
knew that we were on leave

00:39:57.540 --> 00:39:58.970
and who brought casseroles.

00:39:58.970 --> 00:40:03.360
And that whole thing of just like,

00:40:03.360 --> 00:40:04.973
it is treated very differently.

00:40:07.530 --> 00:40:10.920
But there was a point at which
I'm not at that point anymore

00:40:10.920 --> 00:40:13.690
and I'm not comparing depression to those,

00:40:13.690 --> 00:40:15.170
I'm not saying what I
have is the same thing

00:40:15.170 --> 00:40:17.310
what my brother had, you know I mean?

00:40:17.310 --> 00:40:19.060
The trajectories are less certain,

00:40:19.060 --> 00:40:22.470
there are all sorts of major differences.

00:40:22.470 --> 00:40:24.850
But there was a point where like my life

00:40:24.850 --> 00:40:28.970
was in severe jeopardy if
I didn't have treatment.

00:40:28.970 --> 00:40:31.490
And so I think really sharing that

00:40:31.490 --> 00:40:32.810
and getting that message out,

00:40:32.810 --> 00:40:34.730
I think to put some urgency behind it,

00:40:34.730 --> 00:40:38.030
of like, one, you need
access to treatment.

00:40:38.030 --> 00:40:40.560
And two, we need access
to better treatments.

00:40:40.560 --> 00:40:42.010
Because what about those of us

00:40:42.010 --> 00:40:46.960
who aren't responding
to what's already there?

00:40:46.960 --> 00:40:48.783
And I think the other thing,

00:40:50.223 --> 00:40:52.800
I comment on quite a few
things in this article

00:40:52.800 --> 00:40:56.190
about research and how can
we make it more beneficial

00:40:56.190 --> 00:40:58.640
to participants who sign up.

00:40:58.640 --> 00:41:00.110
And I have some suggestions for that.

00:41:00.110 --> 00:41:02.966
And I know, the interventional
psychiatry services

00:41:02.966 --> 00:41:07.966
has taken some of those suggestions.

00:41:10.240 --> 00:41:13.190
But the funding and I think
reimbursement for care.

00:41:13.190 --> 00:41:14.990
And like Gerry said, it's very expensive.

00:41:14.990 --> 00:41:17.860
I mean, I still receive free care,

00:41:17.860 --> 00:41:22.690
which, I have a lot of feelings about.

00:41:22.690 --> 00:41:25.323
Really complicated feelings about.

00:41:26.226 --> 00:41:28.440
But definitely gratitude for sure,

00:41:28.440 --> 00:41:30.440
but lots of complicated feelings.

00:41:30.440 --> 00:41:33.620
And here I am, I told you
I had some health issues

00:41:33.620 --> 00:41:36.960
in these past few years
and one of the medications,

00:41:36.960 --> 00:41:39.030
one of the treatments I might take

00:41:39.030 --> 00:41:42.690
is gonna be $10,000 a month,

00:41:42.690 --> 00:41:44.690
which is much more than
my ketamine bill is,

00:41:44.690 --> 00:41:46.660
but my insurance is gonna pay for it

00:41:48.810 --> 00:41:50.270
without much question of like,

00:41:50.270 --> 00:41:52.630
once these things are proven
and we have these labs

00:41:52.630 --> 00:41:55.560
to say this, you can get this treatment

00:41:55.560 --> 00:41:58.210
that's very expensive and
like have access to it.

00:41:58.210 --> 00:42:01.430
Whereas like we have, I don't
know how many years of data,

00:42:01.430 --> 00:42:03.990
I've probably had 130
ketamine treatments and like,

00:42:03.990 --> 00:42:05.690
we have very good data that show

00:42:05.690 --> 00:42:10.563
that when Ashley can get this
treatment, she is pretty well.

00:42:12.080 --> 00:42:16.003
And still can't make that case.

00:42:18.318 --> 00:42:20.350
So I don't think that necessarily
answered the question.

00:42:20.350 --> 00:42:22.700
I'm trying to remember what it was, but.

00:42:22.700 --> 00:42:23.533
- That's still helpful.

00:42:23.533 --> 00:42:25.353
I mean, that's the context for all it.

00:42:27.550 --> 00:42:30.230
- Yeah, I think Ashley highlights

00:42:30.230 --> 00:42:33.262
many of the biggest problems we face,

00:42:33.262 --> 00:42:38.262
but the issue of providing affordable,

00:42:38.630 --> 00:42:43.630
efficient and effective
behavioral health care is immense.

00:42:44.080 --> 00:42:45.340
I mean, it's huge.

00:42:45.340 --> 00:42:49.890
And I don't think it's possible
to target it at one spot.

00:42:49.890 --> 00:42:52.170
It really is the whole system.

00:42:52.170 --> 00:42:55.014
We need to be able to
have early recognition.

00:42:55.014 --> 00:42:57.240
We need to have access to treatment.

00:42:57.240 --> 00:42:59.430
We need to have what we
call the appropriate level

00:42:59.430 --> 00:43:01.140
or step-based care.

00:43:01.140 --> 00:43:03.163
'Cause as we talked about depression,

00:43:03.163 --> 00:43:05.230
it's a very heterogeneous illness,

00:43:05.230 --> 00:43:07.460
not only heterogeneous it's one

00:43:07.460 --> 00:43:08.760
that the evidence is pretty clear.

00:43:08.760 --> 00:43:10.380
If you can address it early,

00:43:10.380 --> 00:43:14.100
you could prevent later
progression to more severe

00:43:14.100 --> 00:43:16.503
and treatment resistant stages.

00:43:17.950 --> 00:43:22.044
And then obviously for
the unfortunate people

00:43:22.044 --> 00:43:23.450
that don't have early responses

00:43:23.450 --> 00:43:26.270
that we have access to these
really cutting edge treatments.

00:43:26.270 --> 00:43:30.950
And it's hard to do one
of those in isolation.

00:43:30.950 --> 00:43:35.320
Really, the demand is just so much greater

00:43:35.320 --> 00:43:37.593
than the supply of the system.

00:43:38.440 --> 00:43:43.140
It's just impossible for every
person who needs to be seen

00:43:43.140 --> 00:43:46.200
by a behavioral health
specialist to be seen.

00:43:46.200 --> 00:43:49.400
There's just not enough around.

00:43:49.400 --> 00:43:52.510
And especially for everybody to be seen

00:43:53.580 --> 00:43:55.340
by fully trained psychiatrists

00:43:55.340 --> 00:43:59.150
but there's appropriate levels of care

00:43:59.150 --> 00:44:04.020
that can be initiated and
the early you can do that,

00:44:04.020 --> 00:44:07.930
the less expensive or
less costly those are.

00:44:07.930 --> 00:44:10.950
So it's a major overhaul of the system

00:44:10.950 --> 00:44:13.294
that needs to be done.

00:44:13.294 --> 00:44:14.680
I think we're getting there.

00:44:14.680 --> 00:44:17.210
And as I said, I'm trying to infuse

00:44:17.210 --> 00:44:20.670
a little optimism to that
I think people really care.

00:44:20.670 --> 00:44:25.670
And I think even people think,
the insurance companies,

00:44:25.685 --> 00:44:29.885
they're just, I can say when
I talk to the individuals

00:44:29.885 --> 00:44:31.830
that people do care.

00:44:31.830 --> 00:44:36.560
Just how do you make this work fiscally.

00:44:36.560 --> 00:44:39.230
It's not an easy solution, but
it's something we need to do.

00:44:39.230 --> 00:44:41.340
And I think now more than ever,

00:44:41.340 --> 00:44:44.320
where we're seeing that
it's reached a crisis point

00:44:45.480 --> 00:44:47.850
especially for psychiatric disorders,

00:44:47.850 --> 00:44:49.010
especially for depression,

00:44:49.010 --> 00:44:51.820
that the impact that's
having on the country,

00:44:51.820 --> 00:44:54.200
that the burden on the country.

00:44:54.200 --> 00:44:57.720
Both economically, but
even just personal pain

00:44:57.720 --> 00:45:01.228
is just not tolerable.

00:45:01.228 --> 00:45:03.130
- Yeah, definitely agree with you.

00:45:03.130 --> 00:45:04.410
And although people may feel

00:45:04.410 --> 00:45:05.850
like we're not giving a lot of answers.

00:45:05.850 --> 00:45:08.100
I think part of it is
just the awareness too.

00:45:09.090 --> 00:45:11.910
Because for those of us and
I put myself tangentially

00:45:11.910 --> 00:45:14.730
in that category, but in the field,

00:45:14.730 --> 00:45:16.470
conversations that we've
been having for a while,

00:45:16.470 --> 00:45:20.030
but as much of these
conversations need to be expanded

00:45:20.030 --> 00:45:23.330
and enlarged and in scope so
that the system can be changed,

00:45:23.330 --> 00:45:24.890
because just in small pockets,

00:45:24.890 --> 00:45:27.480
that's not gonna do it as
you were alluding to as well.

00:45:27.480 --> 00:45:28.313
So I think it's brilliant.

00:45:28.313 --> 00:45:29.840
- I mean, these are issues

00:45:29.840 --> 00:45:32.370
that are nationwide in
terms of reimbursement

00:45:32.370 --> 00:45:35.900
and in terms of how do you
bring these novel treatments

00:45:35.900 --> 00:45:37.453
to actual clinical care.

00:45:39.440 --> 00:45:42.220
Like you, I grew up, my background's

00:45:42.220 --> 00:45:43.530
in physiology and biophysics.

00:45:43.530 --> 00:45:47.700
I'm a pretty basic scientist at heart.

00:45:47.700 --> 00:45:50.330
And I always had the idea
that if we understood

00:45:50.330 --> 00:45:53.910
the basic physiology, we
could develop good treatments.

00:45:53.910 --> 00:45:55.540
The rest is easy.

00:45:55.540 --> 00:46:00.070
Now, being in the field for 25, 26 years,

00:46:00.070 --> 00:46:03.700
I realized that the basic science
is actually the easy part.

00:46:03.700 --> 00:46:06.760
It's the implementation science

00:46:06.760 --> 00:46:08.030
that's a lot more difficult.

00:46:08.030 --> 00:46:09.700
Showing that these
treatments are effective,

00:46:09.700 --> 00:46:11.710
determining who they're effective for,

00:46:11.710 --> 00:46:14.330
and then getting it, even
once it crosses the goal line,

00:46:14.330 --> 00:46:16.640
how do you actually get it to patients?

00:46:16.640 --> 00:46:18.613
These are some real struggles.

00:46:20.790 --> 00:46:22.930
- I think the other
thing I didn't mention,

00:46:22.930 --> 00:46:25.080
I'll be mad at myself
if I don't bring up,.

00:46:25.920 --> 00:46:27.700
I've talked about treatments
and access treatments,

00:46:27.700 --> 00:46:28.783
better treatments, those things.

00:46:28.783 --> 00:46:30.330
I think the other thing that I talk about

00:46:30.330 --> 00:46:32.390
in my article, and I tried
to talk about all the time.

00:46:32.390 --> 00:46:33.630
And if you sit down with me,

00:46:33.630 --> 00:46:35.610
we're gonna have a long
conversation about it,

00:46:35.610 --> 00:46:38.680
but it's about also the
importance of the relationship

00:46:38.680 --> 00:46:41.005
between a doctor and the patient

00:46:41.005 --> 00:46:46.005
and how in the system that we have built,

00:46:47.190 --> 00:46:50.513
health constrained that becomes.

00:46:52.318 --> 00:46:55.260
And really, I think the burden
that the healthcare system

00:46:55.260 --> 00:46:59.430
places on doctors is
tremendous and on their time,

00:46:59.430 --> 00:47:01.397
and they're reimbursed,
like all of these things.

00:47:01.397 --> 00:47:04.980
And in turn, how that
impacts the relationship

00:47:04.980 --> 00:47:07.970
with the patients and the communication.

00:47:07.970 --> 00:47:11.380
And how, there are things

00:47:11.380 --> 00:47:13.320
that I remember that Gerry said to me

00:47:13.320 --> 00:47:15.950
while I was in the trial that I'm like,

00:47:15.950 --> 00:47:18.540
I will forever hold that in my mind.

00:47:18.540 --> 00:47:21.010
That I've had other
physicians who say things

00:47:21.010 --> 00:47:22.710
they think really communicating,

00:47:22.710 --> 00:47:24.720
being very clear to your patients.

00:47:24.720 --> 00:47:27.330
Especially if they're not
responding to treatments

00:47:27.330 --> 00:47:29.475
or you're in a position where it's like,

00:47:29.475 --> 00:47:30.308
it's really hard to figure out.

00:47:30.308 --> 00:47:32.610
You can be honest about that,
but to really communicate,

00:47:32.610 --> 00:47:36.873
like, I am not giving up on
you and I'm in this with you.

00:47:40.566 --> 00:47:42.210
And I have a team of psychiatrists.

00:47:42.210 --> 00:47:44.630
And some of them, I get to hear them talk

00:47:44.630 --> 00:47:45.670
because I'm at the university.

00:47:45.670 --> 00:47:47.810
And so they're out
there talking, I get to.

00:47:47.810 --> 00:47:52.603
And one of my physicians was
giving a lecture and someone,

00:47:53.630 --> 00:47:55.870
there's a mixed audience
of like the public

00:47:55.870 --> 00:47:57.610
and activist and neuroscientist.

00:47:57.610 --> 00:47:59.520
All of a sudden someone spoke up of like,

00:47:59.520 --> 00:48:02.360
what about someone who's
tried 20 medications?

00:48:02.360 --> 00:48:04.510
What do you say to them?

00:48:04.510 --> 00:48:07.590
And he said, no one has tried everything

00:48:07.590 --> 00:48:10.020
and I will never give up on a patient.

00:48:10.020 --> 00:48:13.530
And I mean, I believed him 100%.

00:48:13.530 --> 00:48:14.790
Not only cause he's my doctor,

00:48:14.790 --> 00:48:17.923
but that is for sure where he's at.

00:48:17.923 --> 00:48:22.923
But how much longer that
can help someone hold on

00:48:25.880 --> 00:48:27.910
and just give a little
more hope where it's like,

00:48:27.910 --> 00:48:30.900
you're holding the hope for
the person who can't hold it.

00:48:30.900 --> 00:48:34.740
That is very, very important.

00:48:34.740 --> 00:48:37.280
And sometimes, there isn't anything to do.

00:48:37.280 --> 00:48:39.510
Sometimes there's just in that moment,

00:48:39.510 --> 00:48:40.740
nothing that can be done,

00:48:40.740 --> 00:48:44.460
but I think acknowledging and
sitting down with the person

00:48:44.460 --> 00:48:46.630
and their suffering
and really saying like,

00:48:46.630 --> 00:48:49.423
I understand what it is
I'm asking you to do.

00:48:50.470 --> 00:48:54.880
I understand by asking you to
stay around what that means.

00:48:54.880 --> 00:48:57.450
'Cause I'm gonna, I'm listening to you.

00:48:57.450 --> 00:48:58.710
I'm feeling it.

00:48:58.710 --> 00:49:01.610
I'm believing you rather than just like,

00:49:01.610 --> 00:49:05.363
this kind of, well, we'll
get around to it next time.

00:49:06.340 --> 00:49:09.160
Or I don't know what to do and sorry,

00:49:09.160 --> 00:49:11.170
but I think really being in it

00:49:11.170 --> 00:49:15.583
with the person communicating
that is, that is medicine.

00:49:16.670 --> 00:49:19.277
- Yeah, and it's hard in its
truest form and so powerful.

00:49:19.277 --> 00:49:20.890
And the fact that those--

00:49:20.890 --> 00:49:23.900
- Probably there's hope in medicine too.

00:49:23.900 --> 00:49:25.840
- Yeah, yeah.

00:49:25.840 --> 00:49:26.800
Very, very true.

00:49:26.800 --> 00:49:28.387
I'm sure we knew we could,
if we had more time,

00:49:28.387 --> 00:49:30.830
we can talk about the neuroscience of that

00:49:30.830 --> 00:49:33.300
and what that's doing to our brains

00:49:33.300 --> 00:49:35.200
and that the interaction there.

00:49:35.200 --> 00:49:36.900
- I would love to sit
down another time, Nii

00:49:36.900 --> 00:49:39.950
and go through that 'cause
that would be a great topic.

00:49:39.950 --> 00:49:41.590
- Yeah.

00:49:41.590 --> 00:49:43.360
Well, I'm grateful to both of you, I mean,

00:49:43.360 --> 00:49:46.330
I feel like we talk through
a lot of important topics,

00:49:46.330 --> 00:49:48.860
but then also just touch the
surface in some ways too.

00:49:48.860 --> 00:49:49.750
There's so much there.

00:49:49.750 --> 00:49:53.520
So definitely be happy to revisit

00:49:53.520 --> 00:49:55.350
in some shape or form as well.

00:49:55.350 --> 00:49:57.246
But to close, I did wanna
just ask both of you,

00:49:57.246 --> 00:50:01.420
to share what gives you hope in this work.

00:50:01.420 --> 00:50:03.410
And I know you both lose
that a little bit as well.

00:50:03.410 --> 00:50:04.830
And then also, if there are any resources

00:50:04.830 --> 00:50:06.110
that you want to share with listeners.

00:50:06.110 --> 00:50:08.010
Ashley, we'll be sure to
actually put your piece

00:50:08.010 --> 00:50:11.429
on the website too, so people
can access it pretty easily.

00:50:11.429 --> 00:50:12.387
- Thank you.

00:50:12.387 --> 00:50:13.220
- But Gerry, we'll start with you

00:50:13.220 --> 00:50:14.580
and then have Ashley wrap up.

00:50:15.490 --> 00:50:19.280
- So I do think for
depression that the advances

00:50:19.280 --> 00:50:21.040
that have been made in the past 20 years

00:50:21.040 --> 00:50:22.780
have been quite amazing.

00:50:22.780 --> 00:50:26.200
The neurobiology, I think is decades ahead

00:50:26.200 --> 00:50:28.350
of the clinical care.

00:50:28.350 --> 00:50:33.320
In our understanding of some
of the basic neuro-biological

00:50:34.430 --> 00:50:37.920
underpinnings of what we're talking about

00:50:37.920 --> 00:50:42.210
when we talk about depression,
it's not homogeneous on this.

00:50:42.210 --> 00:50:44.530
There's many different pathologies

00:50:44.530 --> 00:50:46.277
or ways that you can get there.

00:50:46.277 --> 00:50:48.400
And I think we're starting
to make real strides

00:50:48.400 --> 00:50:49.350
in understanding that.

00:50:49.350 --> 00:50:54.070
And if you think about the
availability of novel treatments,

00:50:54.070 --> 00:50:59.070
I mean, really up until
the last few years,

00:50:59.700 --> 00:51:02.210
almost every medication that
was available for depression

00:51:02.210 --> 00:51:04.680
was based on the
mononeuropathy hypothesis.

00:51:04.680 --> 00:51:08.980
This very hypothesis that was
developed in the early '60s

00:51:08.980 --> 00:51:12.340
suggesting that
norepinephrine and serotonin,

00:51:12.340 --> 00:51:13.630
to some extent, dopamine,

00:51:13.630 --> 00:51:15.787
the basic model of
immunologic neurotransmitters

00:51:15.787 --> 00:51:17.863
are the core of depression.

00:51:19.260 --> 00:51:22.250
The neuroscience, for 40 years,

00:51:22.250 --> 00:51:24.480
we've known that that could not account

00:51:24.480 --> 00:51:27.074
for the basic pathology of depression,

00:51:27.074 --> 00:51:29.300
the pathophysiology and the treatment.

00:51:29.300 --> 00:51:31.640
What we've learned so much that now

00:51:31.640 --> 00:51:34.320
in the past five to 10 years,

00:51:34.320 --> 00:51:37.906
novel treatments have
been approved by the FDA

00:51:37.906 --> 00:51:39.840
that do not target those
systems in any way,

00:51:39.840 --> 00:51:44.130
such as S ketamine, version
of ketamine for depression

00:51:44.130 --> 00:51:46.820
and another drug target
targeting the gabaergic system

00:51:46.820 --> 00:51:49.270
for depression, for postpartum depression.

00:51:49.270 --> 00:51:51.160
And many new drugs in the pipeline.

00:51:51.160 --> 00:51:53.840
So we've sort of broken out of that box

00:51:53.840 --> 00:51:57.720
that's constrained us for
nearly 50 years and opened up

00:51:57.720 --> 00:52:01.060
a whole new vista of ways
of developing treatments.

00:52:01.060 --> 00:52:01.893
But beyond that,

00:52:01.893 --> 00:52:05.730
we've also developed novel
neuro stimulation treatments,

00:52:05.730 --> 00:52:06.829
TMS treatments.

00:52:06.829 --> 00:52:09.600
For years we've had ECT, but that was it.

00:52:09.600 --> 00:52:11.870
Now we're really trying to find ways

00:52:11.870 --> 00:52:14.334
to refine those treatments.

00:52:14.334 --> 00:52:16.760
And we've also broken out
of the box of just accepting

00:52:16.760 --> 00:52:20.110
the fact that it takes weeks to months

00:52:20.110 --> 00:52:21.600
before somebody can benefit.

00:52:21.600 --> 00:52:25.090
And Ashley can tell you one
day struggling with this

00:52:25.090 --> 00:52:26.670
feels like an eternity.

00:52:26.670 --> 00:52:29.340
I mean, when you tell somebody,
well, you'll feel better,

00:52:29.340 --> 00:52:31.520
but you're gonna probably feel
worse before you feel better.

00:52:31.520 --> 00:52:33.180
And it may take two months

00:52:33.180 --> 00:52:37.210
before it's clinically noticeable.

00:52:37.210 --> 00:52:38.230
We've broken out of that.

00:52:38.230 --> 00:52:40.050
Now we know there are
treatments that can make people

00:52:40.050 --> 00:52:42.140
feel better in a much
shorter period of time.

00:52:42.140 --> 00:52:46.000
We still have a long way
to go to improve upon that.

00:52:46.000 --> 00:52:51.000
But I think the advances
are quite real and tangible.

00:52:51.640 --> 00:52:54.510
And I think the next few
decades will bring many more.

00:52:54.510 --> 00:52:56.190
And I think the next few years

00:52:56.190 --> 00:52:59.130
will actually help us
refine what we do have.

00:52:59.130 --> 00:53:00.600
- Yeah, that's definitely encouraging.

00:53:00.600 --> 00:53:02.770
